              "review_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vh__2V2tXUM&t=39s",
              "start_seconds": 39,
              "end_seconds": 191,
              "label": "Collect variable positional arguments into a tuple with *args",
              "summary": "An addition function with two parameters rejects a third positional argument. Replacing those parameters with *args lets the function collect its positional arguments into a tuple. The function then iterates over that tuple, accumulates a total, and returns the sum, allowing calls with different numbers of arguments. The collecting parameter can have another name, such as nums; args is the conventional choice.",
              "fact_ids": [
                "f1"
              ],
              "transcript_ref": "Vh__2V2tXUM:39-191"
            }
          ],
          "group_title": "Collecting arguments and expanding them at a call",
          "bridge": {
            "before": "Watch the first clip expand arguments at a call and the second collect them in a definition. “Splat” means asterisk; the second calls it an “unpacking operator,” but its demonstrated definition behavior is collection.",
            "after": "The first clip teaches call expansion; the second teaches positional collection. Neither covers keyword collection in definitions, duplicate bindings, or forwarding wrappers. The text supplies those rules and a complete forwarding example."
